 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "More pronouns: they are family words",
     "texto": [
      "Many Vietnamese forms of address come from words for family relationships. They do not exactly match «I» and «you»: they are chosen according to the relationship, the ages, the situation and, sometimes, the region.",
      "**anh** (older brother) is usually used to address a man somewhat older or of similar age; whoever uses it can refer to themselves as **anh** when speaking to someone younger.",
      "**chị** (older sister) works in a similar way when addressing a woman; it can also be the way to say «I» in that relationship.",
      "**em** (younger sibling) is usually used to address someone younger. A younger person can use it to refer to themselves when speaking to an older person.",
      "**cô** (aunt) can address a woman of your parents' generation or be used as a respectful form of address, depending on the situation.",
      "**họ** = they.",
      "**ấy** forms the 3rd person: **anh ấy** = he, **cô ấy / chị ấy** = she, **em ấy** = he or she (younger).",
      "They can be used alone or with a name: «**cô Phương**», «**anh Jack**». **tôi** is a useful option for talking about yourself in formal situations or with people you don't know well; the right choice depends on the context."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
